lesson g6-speed-distance-and-time v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=4 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Speed, distance and time, and the distance-time graph"
  goal "Find a speed, a distance or a time from the other two, and read a speed off a distance-time graph."

  look {
    scene 35x20 {
      linegraph graph labels=["start", "1 h", "2 h", "3 h"] values=[0, 12, 24, 36] min=0 max=45 per=3 step=9 color=sky at=canvas(1, 1)
      ropebridge bridge planks=10 gaps=0 right-of=graph gap=2
    }
    say """
      A cyclist rides from the mill along the gorge road. The graph shows how far she has gone after
      each hour: 12 km, then 24, then 36, so her speed is 12 km in each hour, 12 km/h. Speed is the
      distance gone in one unit of time. Distance is speed times time, and time is distance shared
      by the speed. On a distance-time graph a steeper line is a faster speed, and a flat line is a
      rest.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice speed.cyclist-gorge-road count=1 seed=3
    level easy {
      worked speed.boat-down-river v=8 m=90 level=medium
    }
    practice speed.boat-down-river count=1 seed=4
    show speed.kingfisher-bridge s=8 t=12 easy-s=10 easy-t=6 hard-s=12 hard-t=20
    practice speed.cyclist-graph-hour count=1 seed=6
    show speed.which-canoe-faster a=9 t1=1.5 b=14 t2=2 easy-a=15 easy-t1=3 easy-b=10 easy-t2=2.5 hard-a=15 hard-t1=3 hard-b=14 hard-t2=2
    show speed.river-metres-per-second v=12 d=0 easy-v=5 hard-v=8 hard-d=1
    practice speed.boat-down-river count=1 seed=8 hard-seed=21
    level hard {
      show speed.cyclist-graph-hour p=12 q=0 r=8 k=2
    }
    show speed.kingfisher-bridge s=12 t=8 easy-s=10 easy-t=9 hard-s=15 hard-t=12
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show speed.round-trip-mistake s=1 easy-s=0 hard-s=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show speed.catch-the-cyclist a=12 g=4 h=30 easy-g=6 hard-a=15 hard-g=3 hard-h=20
  }
  remember {
    say "Speed is the distance gone in one hour or one second. Distance is speed times time, and time is distance shared by the speed."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The three facts are one fact seen three ways, and a double number line with kilometres on top
    and hours underneath shows all of them. The common slips are mixing minutes with hours (8 km/h
    for 30 minutes is 4 km, not 240) and multiplying when the time is the unknown, so ask each time
    what one hour, or one second, is worth. On the graph, the speed in one hour is how far the line
    climbs in that hour, not how high it is. In the two-star question the boat spends longer on the
    slow way, so the average is below halfway; total distance over total time settles it. In the
    three-star question Kit closes the gap only by the difference in speeds. If minutes and hours
    still tangle, stay here another day with whole hours. Off the page, time a walk to the shop and
    back and work out the speed in metres per second.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the rides take 2 hours, a boat trip of 90 minutes is worked in the teacher's pen, the boat goes for whole hours or half an hour, the kingfisher flies 10 m/s, the graph's first hour is asked, the canoe that went further is also the faster, 5 m/s is changed to km/h, the two-star boat goes 3 and 6 km/h, and Kit is 6 km/h faster with a 30-minute gap."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the rides take 4 hours, the boat goes for 20, 40 or 45 minutes, the kingfisher flies 12 or 15 m/s, the graph is asked twice with a rest in it, the canoe that went further is the slower, the speed is changed from km/h to m/s, the two-star boat goes 6 and 12 km/h over 24 km, and Kit is only 3 km/h faster at 15 and 18 km/h with a 20-minute gap."
  }
}
